include "opengrep";

# fixture: fixtures/java/weak-random.java
# ported-from: java/lang/security/audit/crypto/weak-random.yaml (weak-random)
#
# CWE-330. java.util.Random is a linear congruential generator: watch a couple
# of outputs and you know the rest, which is fine for a shuffle and not for
# anything a stranger must not guess.
#
# The original writes the class fully qualified - `new java.util.Random(...)` -
# and Semgrep resolves the import to make that match `new Random()`. pwrq does
# not resolve anything: the pattern is matched as written, so the port names
# the spelling that appears in the file. That is a real difference and worth
# stating plainly - a class imported under an alias will not be found.

["new Random($$$_).$FUNC($$$_)", "Math.random()"] as $guessable
| scan("*.java"; $guessable)
| finding("java-weak-random";
    "\(.Text) is predictable from a few earlier outputs; SecureRandom for anything "
    + "that has to be unguessable")
| report
